92186045417 true] [17592186437134 1075 #edb/ref 17592186437130 13194139534823 17592186045417 true] [17592186437134 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 13194139534823 17592186045417 true] [17592186437134 1077 "selection" 13194139534823 17592186045417 true] [17592186437134 1078 #edb/ref 17592186437112 13194139534823 17592186045417 true] [17592186437134 1079 #uuid "8aa4c72e-18f1-5153-bc4d-2cd8d8207293" 13194139534823 17592186045417 true] [17592186437135 1075 #edb/ref 17592186437113 13194139534823 17592186045417 true] [17592186437135 1075 #edb/ref 17592186437119 13194139534823 17592186045417 true] [17592186437135 1075 #edb/ref 17592186437121 13194139534823 17592186045417 true] [17592186437135 1075 #edb/ref 17592186437125 13194139534823 17592186045417 true] [17592186437135 1075 #edb/ref 17592186437128 13194139534823 17592186045417 true] [17592186437135 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 13194139534823 17592186045417 true] [17592186437135 1077 "selection" 13194139534823 17592186045417 true] [17592186437135 1078 #edb/ref 17592186437119 13194139534823 17592186045417 true] [17592186437135 1079 #uuid "c35bc065-fcec-52cb-96c4-b81be5324354" 13194139534823 17592186045417 true] [17592186437136 1068 #edb/ref 17592186437135 13194139534823 17592186045417 true] [17592186437136 1069 "q-131102" 13194139534823 17592186045417 true] [17592186437136 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139534823 17592186045417 true] [17592186437136 1071 "A company rents cars to tourists. On average, there have been $3$ rental requests each day. What is the probability that none of the cars are rented the next day? Round your answer to $3$ decimal places. You may assume that rental requests occur independently and at a constant average rate." 13194139534823 17592186045417 true] [17592186437136 1072 #uuid "033d05af-1912-5a6e-86f3-f9dda400af0a" 13194139534823 17592186045417 true] [17592186437136 1074 "A Poisson random variable $X$ with rate $\\lambda $ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{{\\lambda }^{x}{e}^{-\\lambda }}{x!}\n$$\n\nLet $X$ be the number of cars rented in a day. Since the average rate is $3$ requests per day, and events occur independently and at a constant average rate, the number of rented cars can be modeled as a Poisson random variable with rate $\\lambda =3,$ and we can write $X∼\\text{Po}(3).$\n\nSo, $X$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{{3}^{x}{e}^{-3}}{x!}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the probability that none of the cars are rented the next day is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X=0) & =f(0) \\\\  & =\\frac{{3}^{0}{e}^{-3}}{0!} \\\\  & ≈0.050\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places." 13194139534823 17592186045417 true] [17592186437137 1068 #edb/ref 17592186437134 13194139534823 17592186045417 true] [17592186437137 1069 "q-131276" 13194139534823 17592186045417 true] [17592186437137 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139534823 17592186045417 true] [17592186437137 1071 "The mean number of bacteria per milliliter of a liquid is $6.$ What is the probability that there will be no more than $3$ bacteria in one milliliter of the liquid? Round your answer to $3$ decimal places. You may assume that bacteria appear independently and at a constant average rate." 13194139534823 17592186045417 true] [17592186437137 1072 #uuid "57e8cbd7-ad98-573e-bd99-c98a3e1a7b22" 13194139534823 17592186045417 true] [17592186437137 1074 "A Poisson random variable $X$ with rate $\\lambda $ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{{\\lambda }^{x}{e}^{-\\lambda }}{x!}\n$$\n\nLet $X$ be the number of bacteria in one milliliter of the liquid. Since the average rate is $6$ bacteria per milliliter, and events occur independently and at a constant average rate, the number of bacteria can be modeled as a Poisson random variable with rate $\\lambda =6,$ and we can write $X∼\\text{Po}(6).$\n\nSo, $X$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{{6}^{x}{e}^{-6}}{x!}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the probability that at most $3$ bacteria will be found in one milliliter of the liquid is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 3) & =P(X\\in \\{0,1,2,3\\}) \\\\  & =f(0)+f(1)+f(2)+f(3) \\\\  & =\\frac{{6}^{0}{e}^{-6}}{0!}+\\frac{{6}^{1}{e}^{-6}}{1!}+\\frac{{6}^{2}{e}^{-6}}{2!}+\\frac{{6}^{3}{e}^{-6}}{3!} \\\\  & ≈0.151\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places." 13194139534823 17592186045417 true] [17592186437138 1068 #edb/ref 17592186437133 13194139534823 17592186045417 true] [17592186437138 1069 "q-85394" 13194139534823 17592186045417 true] [17592186437138 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139534823 17592186045417 true] [17592186437138 1071 "A telephone exchange receives an average of $7$ calls every $5$ minutes. What is the probability that the exchange will receive at least $5$ calls in the next $5$ minutes? Round your answer to $3$ decimal places. You may assume that calls occur independently and at a constant average rate." 13194139534823 17592186045417 true] [17592186437138 1072 #uuid "630f14f8-eb57-54c4-9fbf-4988a90b57a0" 13194139534823 17592186045417 true] [17592186437138 1074 "A Poisson random variable $X$ with rate $\\lambda $ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{{\\lambda }^{x}{e}^{-\\lambda }}{x!}\n$$\n\nLet $X$ be the number of calls received in the telephone exchange. Since the average rate is $7$ calls every $5$ minutes, and events occur independently and at a constant average rate, the number of calls can be modeled as a Poisson random variable with rate $\\lambda =7,$ and we can write $X∼\\text{Po}(7).$ \n\nSo, $X$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{{7}^{x}{e}^{-7}}{x!}\n$$\n\nTo compute $P(X\\ge 5),$ we will use the complement:\n\n$$\nP(X\\ge 5)=1-P(X<5)\n$$\n\nComputing the probability of the complement, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X<5) & =P(X\\in \\{0,1,2,3,4\\}) \\\\  & =f(0)+f(1)+f(2)+f(3)+f(4) \\\\  & =\\frac{{7}^{0}{e}^{-7}}{0!}+\\frac{{7}^{1}{e}^{-7}}{1!}+\\frac{{7}^{2}{e}^{-7}}{2!}+\\frac{{7}^{3}{e}^{-7}}{3!}+\\frac{{7}^{4}{e}^{-7}}{4!} \\\\  & ≈0.173\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places.\n\nTherefore, the probability of receiving at least $5$ calls in the next $5$ minutes is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\ge 5) & =1-P(X<5) \\\\  & ≈1-0.173 \\\\  & =0.827\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places." 13194139534823 17592186045417 true] [17592186437139 1068 #edb/ref 17592186437132 13194139534823 17592186045417 true] [17592186437139 1069 "q-131281" 13194139534823 17592186045417 true] [17592186437139 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139534823 17592186045417 true] [17592186437139 1071 "Previous results indicate that, on average, for each batch of transistors, $1$ is defective. What is the probability that in the next batch of transistors, there will be less than $4$ defective transistors? Round your answer to $3$ decimal places. You may assume that defective transistors occur independently and at a constant average rate." 13194139534823 17592186045417 true] [17592186437139 1072 #uuid "c5edc979-6530-503b-9c43-a29e848f0191" 13194139534823 17592186045417 true] [17592186437139 1074 "A Poisson random variable $X$ with rate $\\lambda $ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{{\\lambda }^{x}{e}^{-\\lambda }}{x!}\n$$\n\nLet $X$ be the number of defective transistors in a batch of transistors. Since the average rate is $1$ defective transistors per batch, and events occur independently and at a constant average rate, the number of defective transistors can be modeled as a Poisson random variable with rate $\\lambda =1,$ and we can write $X∼\\text{Po}(1).$\n\nSo, $X$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{{1}^{x}{e}^{-1}}{x!}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the probability that in the next batch of transistors, there will be less than $4$ defective transistors is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X<4) & =P(X\\in \\{0,1,2,3\\}) \\\\  & =f(0)+f(1)+f(2)+f(3) \\\\  & =\\frac{{1}^{0}{e}^{-1}}{0!}+\\frac{{1}^{1}{e}^{-1}}{1!}+\\frac{{1}^{2}{e}^{-1}}{2!}+\\frac{{1}^{3}{e}^{-1}}{3!} \\\\  & ≈0.981\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places." 13194139534823 17592186045417 true] [17592186437140 1058 #edb/ref 17592186437144 13194139534823 17592186045417 true] [17592186437140 1062 #uuid "004acfbe-1d4d-5f2f-b440-224bff7b1696" 13194139534823 17592186045417 true] [17592186437140 1063 #edb/ref 17592186437138 13194139534823 17592186045417 true] [17592186437141 1058 #edb/ref 17592186437140 13194139534823 17592186045417 true] [17592186437141 1062 #uuid "212ee944-3799-52f3-82f8-1c83bc934474" 13194139534823 17592186045417 true] [17592186437141 1063 #edb/ref 17592186157854 13194139534823 17592186045417 true] [17592186437142 1058 #edb/ref 17592186437143 13194139534823 17592186045417 true] [17592186437142 1062 #uuid "a6a9e584-e23b-5f27-aa56-050dc0d31fff" 13194139534823 17592186045417 true] [17592186437142 1063 #edb/ref 17592186437137 13194139534823 17592186045417 true] [17592186437143 1062 #uuid "db7e6473-de0a-51cd-a4c0-7c7adca3341e" 13194139534823 17592186045417 true] [17592186437143 1063 #edb/ref 17592186437139 13194139534823 17592186045417 true] [17592186437144 1058 #edb/ref 17592186437142 13194139534823 17592186045417 true] [17592186437144 1062 #uuid "f54fa653-ea98-5ece-9516-abf27f41ad60" 13194139534823 17592186045417 true] [17592186437144 1063 #edb/ref 17592186437136 13194139534823 17592186045417 true]] :edb/tempids {"activity-6e3ee6dd-60a3-557f-8bd4-cac744c68b82" 17592186437111 "answer-0f9e78be-1c05-5746-af99-56ee649b8e69" 17592186437112 "answer-103af267-4448-5f1d-b2c7-5d3a6eb5caa1" 17592186437113 "answer-16527557-36e3-5765-85fe-85ad503bd10f" 17592186437114 "answer-23e97ddf-d3f7-50f6-875a-5463203ee997" 17592186437115 "answer-3cd87632-86c9-568a-93e5-d7ef69437ccc" 17592186437116 "answer-4e7ef5fa-25a0-50e1-8283-427fee96d7fc" 17592186437117 "answer-4f5cd1e1-b1c5-5fc7-9253-2dc3e4f28a46" 17592186437118 "answer-5546866d-cf0c-5f34-a20b-0d37698b92d2" 17592186437119 "answer-6ff4f1e3-cf6e-5cd8-8f0c-fc33cf9a7534" 175921